<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" >
        <title>How Many Calculus Students Does it Take to Change a Light<nbsp/>Bulb?</title>
        <objectives>
                <ul>
                        <li>
                                This activity is designed to stretch students modeling ability by
                                having them solve a realistic optimization problem.
                        </li>
                </ul>
        </objectives>
  <introduction>
    <p>
There is a chandelier in the atrium of the main hall on the campus of Enormous State University that needs to have its light bulbs changed.  To get a ladder into the room, maintenance would have to bring it down a hall and around a corner from the maintenance rooms.  If the ladder is too long, it will get stuck going around the corner.  <xref ref="fig_ladder01" /> shows a diagram of the hallway and corner, and examples of various <q>ladders</q> that might get stuck.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig_ladder01">
            <caption>Two Hallways and Possible <q>Ladders</q></caption>
            <image width="50%">
                    <description><p>A visual depiction of a corner where two hallways of different widths meet with dashed lines from the far wall of one hallway to the inner corner and on to the far wall of the other hallway.</p></description>
                    <xi:include href="./ladder01.xml"/>
            </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>
  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
The narrower hallway is 2<nbsp/>meters wide, and the wider is 5<nbsp/>meters wide.
Maintenance has a really tall employee, Henry, who is tall enough to change the
bulbs with their 9<nbsp/>meter ladder (if he stretches), but anyone else would need
their next size up, which is 10<nbsp/>meters.  
</p>
<p>
The problem is that a bunch of the bulbs just blew out, and they need the bulbs
replaced before the Board of Trustees meets tomorrow. Not knowing that he would
be needed, maintenance already allowed Henry to put in his hours for the week,
so they would have to pay him overtime.  
</p>
<p>
The alternative, unfortunately, is not very attractive, either.  Due to
construction, there is no direct way to access the lights without taking a
ladder outside, going around a couple of other buildings, and so on, to bring
the ladder in the main entrance.  This would cost even more than bringing in
        Henry, because they would have to send <em>two</em> employees to carry the ladder
that far!  (On the other hand, if even the smaller ladder won't fit, they will
have to use this option anyway, so they won't add the extra expense of paying
Henry overtime!)
</p>
<p>
Should they give Henry a call?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
            <p>
                    There are several ways to model the problem based on choice of decision variable. I will outline several below. In all of these, I will treat the top of the image in <xref ref="fig_ladder01" text="type-local" /> as <q>north</q> and imagine a tape measure stretched from a point on the west wall to the north wall touching the inner corner.
            </p>
            <p>
                            Let our decision variable be the horizontal distance from the inner corner to the point where the tape measure touches the north wall, and call it <m>x</m>. The domain would be <m>x\gt 0</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                    We will deal with the length of the tape measure in two sections, one from the inner corner to the north wall, which we will call <m>\ell_{1}</m>, and one from the west wall to the inner corner, which will be <m>\ell_{2}</m>. From the Pythagorean theorem, we have 
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \ell_{1}&amp;=\sqrt{x^{2}+5^{2}}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                            &amp;=\sqrt{x^{2}+25}
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
                    and using similar triangles, <m>\frac{x}{2}=\frac{\ell_{1}}{\ell_{2}}</m>, so 
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \ell_{2}&amp;=\frac{2\ell_{1}}{x}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                            &amp;=\frac{2\sqrt{x^{2}+25}}{x}
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
                    making our total length
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \ell(x)&amp;=\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                    &amp;=\sqrt{x^{2}+25}+\frac{2\sqrt{x^{2}+25}}{x}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                            &amp;=\left(1+\frac{2}{x}\right)\sqrt{x^{2}+25}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                            &amp;=\left(\frac{x+2}{x}\right)\sqrt{x^{2}+25}
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
                            Another way to get the same function is to use different similar triangles and notice that <m>\frac{\ell_{1}}{x}=\frac{\ell}{x+2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                    Similarly, if we use the vertical distance from the inner corner to the point where the tape measure touches the west wall, call it <m>y</m>, with <m>y\gt 0</m>, we obtain
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \ell(y)&amp;=\sqrt{y^{2}+4}+\frac{5\sqrt{y^{2}+4}}{y}
                            </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                                            &amp;=\left(1+\frac{5}{y}\right)\sqrt{y^{2}+4}
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                    Another solution is to use an angle as the decision variable. Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle made by the tape measure and a horizontal line through the point where the tape measure touches the west wall, with domain <m>0\lt \theta \lt \pi/2</m>. Then using <m>\ell_{1}</m> and <m>\ell_{2}</m> as above, we have 
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \sin(\theta)&amp;=\frac{5}{\ell_{1}}
                                    &amp;
                                    \cos(\theta)&amp;=\frac{2}{\ell_{2}}
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
                    so
                    <md>
                            <mrow>
                                    \ell(\theta)&amp;=\frac{5}{\sin(\theta)}+\frac{2}{\cos(\theta)}.
                            </mrow>
                    </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                    Although we do not yet have all the tools to manually calculate the derivatives of these functions, 
            </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
